00:01:08.867 --> 00:01:12.082
And then the last reminder
is that you can get a version

00:01:12.082 --> 00:01:15.632
of this a live stream in podcast format

00:01:15.632 --> 00:01:17.412
however you get your podcasts

00:01:17.412 --> 00:01:20.072
at Homeroom With Sal the podcast.

00:01:20.072 --> 00:01:24.529
So with that I'm really excited
to introduce Neel Kashkari.

00:01:24.529 --> 00:01:26.795
Neel, thanks for joining.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:30.660
- Thanks for having me Sal,
it's great to be with you.

00:01:31.922 --> 00:01:34.542
- So I've been dying to
have this conversation

00:01:34.542 --> 00:01:36.962
since you first came on my radar

00:01:36.962 --> 00:01:41.702
obviously during the 2010 financial crisis

00:01:41.702 --> 00:01:43.095
you were very involved there.

00:01:43.095 --> 00:01:45.765
Then you had a really fascinating,

00:01:47.301 --> 00:01:49.962
I guess career where
now you're the president

00:01:49.962 --> 00:01:52.862
of the Federal Reserve
Bank of Minneapolis.

00:01:52.862 --> 00:01:54.182
So let's start in the present,

00:01:54.182 --> 00:01:58.852
maybe a good place to start
is I think most people

00:01:58.852 --> 00:02:00.525
have a general idea that
there's something called

00:02:00.525 --> 00:02:04.597
the Federal Reserve and affects
things like monetary policy

00:02:04.597 --> 00:02:05.455
and interest rates.

00:02:05.455 --> 00:02:07.832
And then you have these regional banks

00:02:07.832 --> 00:02:09.555
of which you're a
president of one of them.

00:02:09.555 --> 00:02:10.892
How does all of this work?

00:02:10.892 --> 00:02:13.000
What is the overall charter
of the Federal Reserve

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:17.612
and what is kind of the
role of the regional banks?

00:02:17.612 --> 00:02:19.442
- Well, thanks for starting at that Sal.

00:02:19.442 --> 00:02:23.142
So in 1913, Congress created
the Federal Reserve System

00:02:23.142 --> 00:02:24.842
which is the nation's central bank

00:02:24.842 --> 00:02:27.682
and very simply our job [Indistinct]

00:02:27.682 --> 00:02:30.079
interest rates up and down
to try to manage the ups

00:02:30.079 --> 00:02:32.432
and downs of the US economy.

00:02:32.432 --> 00:02:35.402
But Congress were unique in 1913

00:02:35.402 --> 00:02:38.312
they said they don't want the
central bank simply housed

00:02:38.312 --> 00:02:40.252
in Washington, D.C in the capital,

00:02:40.252 --> 00:02:42.512
they wanted it distributed
around the country.

00:02:42.512 --> 00:02:45.372
So they created 12 independent
Federal Reserve Banks,

00:02:45.372 --> 00:02:47.972
the ninth of which is the Minneapolis Fed.

00:02:47.972 --> 00:02:50.672
So our job was to cover
this region, Minnesota

00:02:50.672 --> 00:02:53.452
North and South Dakota,
Montana, part of Michigan,

00:02:53.452 --> 00:02:56.079
part of Wisconsin and the
12th Federal Reserve Bank is

00:02:56.079 --> 00:02:58.292
the Federal Reserve Bank of San Francisco

00:02:58.292 --> 00:03:02.237
which covers a huge area that
wasn't very populated in 1913

00:03:02.237 --> 00:03:04.402
but today it's really populated.

00:03:04.402 --> 00:03:06.612
The whole Western third of United States

00:03:06.612 --> 00:03:09.492
is effectively covered
by the San Francisco Fed.

00:03:09.492 --> 00:03:11.262
So our jobs are, we do a bunch of things,

00:03:11.262 --> 00:03:14.012
we regulate banks, we provide cash.

00:03:14.012 --> 00:03:16.482
If you go to the ATM and need
to take out a hundred dollars

00:03:16.482 --> 00:03:19.502
that cash would have started
out in a Federal Reserve Bank

00:03:19.502 --> 00:03:22.812
but we also understand what's
happening in the economy

00:03:22.812 --> 00:03:24.229
and participate in the process

00:03:24.229 --> 00:03:26.802
where we set interest rates for the nation

00:03:26.802 --> 00:03:29.412
to try to achieve this
economy that is growing,

00:03:29.412 --> 00:03:32.035
not overheating but
also not limping along.

00:03:33.942 --> 00:03:35.772
- And for anyone who's curious

00:03:35.772 --> 00:03:37.482
we have actually many Khan Academy videos

00:03:37.482 --> 00:03:41.552
on exactly the mechanism
by how the Federal Reserve

00:03:41.552 --> 00:03:44.332
can insert money and
affect interest rates.

00:03:44.332 --> 00:03:46.532
It's a little bit complex,
but definitely understandable,

00:03:46.532 --> 00:03:47.731
so I encourage people to check that out

00:03:47.731 --> 00:03:50.358
if you're really curious about that

00:03:50.358 --> 00:03:53.669
and Neel what is on a day-to-day basis,

00:03:53.669 --> 00:03:55.442
what are kind of the types of questions

00:03:55.442 --> 00:04:00.152
that you think about and are
kind of within your charter?

00:04:00.152 --> 00:04:02.332
I was just thinking about right now.

00:04:02.332 --> 00:04:05.078
- Well, right now I
mean, we have two goals

00:04:05.078 --> 00:04:07.672
fundamental goals that
Congress has given us

00:04:07.672 --> 00:04:09.242
to achieve that stable economy.

00:04:09.242 --> 00:04:13.332
One is low inflation that
we define as 2% inflation,

00:04:13.332 --> 00:04:15.132
and one is maximum employment

00:04:15.132 --> 00:04:17.812
as many Americans household are working

00:04:17.812 --> 00:04:19.742
and participating in our economy.

00:04:19.742 --> 00:04:22.360
And we normally think these
two goals are intention

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:23.959
and we're trading them off of each other.

00:04:23.959 --> 00:04:26.422
So if the economy is really strong

00:04:26.422 --> 00:04:29.062
and businesses have to
compete to find workers

00:04:29.062 --> 00:04:30.932
the unemployment rate will go down,

00:04:30.932 --> 00:04:33.422
wages will go up and
that'll lead to inflation

00:04:33.422 --> 00:04:34.492
and then we'll have to raise it

00:04:34.492 --> 00:04:36.773
through straits to level that off.

00:04:36.773 --> 00:04:39.102
Well, so that's where a
lot of our time is focused,

00:04:39.102 --> 00:04:41.272
Is are we at maximum employment?

00:04:41.272 --> 00:04:42.622
What's happening in the economy?

00:04:42.622 --> 00:04:44.272
Is there spare capacity?

00:04:44.272 --> 00:04:47.516
Now with the pandemic, you
know before the pandemic hit,

00:04:47.516 --> 00:04:49.224
unemployment rate in America was quite low

00:04:49.224 --> 00:04:52.512
at around three and half percent
but then the pandemic hit

00:04:52.512 --> 00:04:55.002
and millions of Americans lost their jobs.

00:04:55.002 --> 00:04:56.882
So the past year my
job has been different,

00:04:56.882 --> 00:04:59.207
for the past year I've
spent a lot of time talking

00:04:59.207 --> 00:05:01.862
to the best health experts in the country

00:05:01.862 --> 00:05:04.942
to understand where is
the virus likely going?

00:05:04.942 --> 00:05:07.062
How long is this crisis going to endure?

00:05:07.062 --> 00:05:09.612
And then what does that mean
for our economic recovery?

00:05:09.612 --> 00:05:12.152
So we can achieve maximum employment

00:05:12.152 --> 00:05:14.265
and put Americans back to work.

00:05:14.265 --> 00:05:16.232
- And what do you think are the levers

00:05:16.232 --> 00:05:18.172
at the Federal Reserves?

00:05:18.172 --> 00:05:20.422
What are the levers that you all disposal?

00:05:20.422 --> 00:05:22.904
There's this notion of
lowering interest rates

00:05:22.904 --> 00:05:25.132
as much as possible and
some of the mechanics

00:05:25.132 --> 00:05:26.742
of that is essentially you're able

00:05:26.742 --> 00:05:30.002
to increase the aggregate
Federal Reserve balance sheet

00:05:30.002 --> 00:05:34.982
insert more cash and so
interest rates can come down,

00:05:34.982 --> 00:05:37.872
but it seems like right now
we're already getting close

00:05:37.872 --> 00:05:40.172
to the floor there.

00:05:40.172 --> 00:05:42.682
Are there other levers that
the Federal Reserve can do

00:05:42.682 --> 00:05:47.372
or is it really more dependent
on the federal government

00:05:47.372 --> 00:05:50.509
to do more fiscal stimulus at this point?

00:05:50.509 --> 00:05:52.162
- Yeah, great, great question.

00:05:52.162 --> 00:05:54.114
So we are doing a lot,
we're using our tools

00:05:54.114 --> 00:05:56.132
I wouldn't say that
they're fully maxed out

00:05:56.132 --> 00:05:57.812
but we're using them aggressively.

00:05:57.812 --> 00:06:00.022
And by lowering interest
rates, we'll make it cheaper

00:06:00.022 --> 00:06:02.522
for a family to get a
mortgage to buy a house

00:06:02.522 --> 00:06:04.861
or for a business to get a
loan to go build a factory.

00:06:04.861 --> 00:06:06.962
That's how we stimulate the economy.

00:06:06.962 --> 00:06:09.218
So our foot on the floor right
now will be trying to give

00:06:09.218 --> 00:06:11.844
a lot of stimulus to get
the economy moving again.

00:06:11.844 --> 00:06:13.152
And you're right.

00:06:13.152 --> 00:06:15.308
So now the key really is
the fiscal policy makers

00:06:15.308 --> 00:06:18.922
or the Congress to continue
to step up to support

00:06:18.922 --> 00:06:21.062
the millions of Americans
have lost their jobs

00:06:21.062 --> 00:06:23.932
and then thousands and
thousands of small businesses.

00:06:23.932 --> 00:06:26.472
Many have already shuttered,
but many are teetering

00:06:26.472 --> 00:06:28.382
because their businesses
have been impacted by this.

00:06:28.382 --> 00:06:33.382
And so the COVID crisis is an
example where monetary policy

00:06:33.392 --> 00:06:36.172
and fiscal policy can
compliment each other

00:06:36.172 --> 00:06:37.837
to support the American people until

00:06:37.837 --> 00:06:39.745
we get the pandemic behind us.

00:06:40.852 --> 00:06:42.692
- And one question related to that,

00:06:42.692 --> 00:06:44.484
that I've been having a dinner parties

00:06:44.484 --> 00:06:45.465
and I don't know the right answer to it.

00:06:45.465 --> 00:06:47.765
I don't think there's a clear answer.

00:06:49.402 --> 00:06:51.152
All of the monetary policy

00:06:51.152 --> 00:06:54.293
and the monetary stimulus
has been going in

00:06:54.293 --> 00:06:55.982
it makes all the sense in the world to me

00:06:55.982 --> 00:06:58.056
for all the reasons you just said

00:06:58.056 --> 00:07:00.392
especially in service sector, retail jobs,

00:07:00.392 --> 00:07:03.452
travel airlines they are suffering.

00:07:03.452 --> 00:07:04.942
If you can lower interest rates,

00:07:04.942 --> 00:07:06.693
it can stimulate some demand,

00:07:06.693 --> 00:07:10.172
people can continue to
invest in certain areas,

00:07:10.172 --> 00:07:11.329
the cost of buying certain things

00:07:11.329 --> 00:07:13.302
will go down, et cetera, et cetera.

00:07:13.302 --> 00:07:15.902
But it does seem like
there's this divergence,

00:07:15.902 --> 00:07:18.872
people have talked about the
K-shaped economic recovery,

00:07:18.872 --> 00:07:23.102
where to some degree those
who do have access to capital

00:07:23.102 --> 00:07:26.342
even people who own houses,
when interest rates go down

00:07:26.342 --> 00:07:28.212
they can refinance they benefit,

00:07:28.212 --> 00:07:30.612
the buyers have more purchasing power

00:07:30.612 --> 00:07:33.322
so the house price goes up they benefit,

00:07:33.322 --> 00:07:35.842
arguably companies might benefit

00:07:35.842 --> 00:07:37.722
and once again, that might have follow

00:07:37.722 --> 00:07:39.782
on benefits for many other folks.

00:07:39.782 --> 00:07:41.845
But if you use the stock
market out of the stock market

00:07:41.845 --> 00:07:44.432
should necessarily be the
high fidelity indicator,

00:07:44.432 --> 00:07:46.125
but if you use that, if
you use other indicators

00:07:46.125 --> 00:07:50.322
that does look like this
separation that's happened

00:07:50.322 --> 00:07:53.552
where those who have some
assets who have access

00:07:53.552 --> 00:07:56.705
to kind of resources are doing better,

00:07:58.431 --> 00:08:01.552
and the monetary policy isn't
able to necessarily reach

00:08:01.552 --> 00:08:04.765
as much those who don't
have as many resources.

00:08:05.732 --> 00:08:07.042
- I think that's very fair.

00:08:07.042 --> 00:08:08.731
I think the K-shaped recovery is real.

00:08:08.731 --> 00:08:12.570
The tech sector is booming, the
companies like in Minnesota,

00:08:12.570 --> 00:08:14.759
General Mills is one of our big companies.

00:08:14.759 --> 00:08:16.602
They grew up, provide the food

00:08:16.602 --> 00:08:18.662
that's on the shelves
in our grocery stores

00:08:18.662 --> 00:08:20.742
because we've all stopped
going out to restaurants

00:08:20.742 --> 00:08:22.390
and I go to the grocery
store a lot more often.

00:08:22.390 --> 00:08:23.872
Companies like General Mills

00:08:23.872 --> 00:08:25.922
are doing really well in this pandemic

00:08:25.922 --> 00:08:27.045
but then you have the restaurants

00:08:27.045 --> 00:08:29.862
and you have the airlines
and you have the hotels

00:08:29.862 --> 00:08:32.662
and so I think the
K-shaped recovery is real.

00:08:32.662 --> 00:08:34.982
And let's be clear, low interest rates

00:08:34.982 --> 00:08:37.672
is not gonna do anything
to help the restaurant

00:08:37.672 --> 00:08:40.492
that is closed because
of the pandemic, right?

00:08:40.492 --> 00:08:41.570
If they can't have customers come in

00:08:41.570 --> 00:08:44.152
because it's not safe,
there's simply nothing

00:08:44.152 --> 00:08:45.782
that interest rates are
gonna do to help them,

00:08:45.782 --> 00:08:48.262
and that's why we're doing whatever we can

00:08:48.262 --> 00:08:51.662
to give the best analysis we have,

00:08:51.662 --> 00:08:53.675
encourage our friends in the Congress

00:08:53.675 --> 00:08:57.502
or in the executive branch
to provide fiscal support

00:08:57.502 --> 00:08:58.682
to people that have been laid off

00:08:58.682 --> 00:09:01.882
into those small businesses
where monetary policy

00:09:01.882 --> 00:09:03.631
is not gonna be very helpful.

00:09:03.631 --> 00:09:06.252
The monetary policy is a blunt instrument

00:09:06.252 --> 00:09:08.872
that affects the whole
economy at the same time.

00:09:08.872 --> 00:09:10.752
There's no way for us to target it

00:09:10.752 --> 00:09:12.805
to one sector or another sector.

00:09:14.057 --> 00:09:15.442
- And what's your view there?

00:09:15.442 --> 00:09:19.125
I know the Federal Reserve
is an intentionally separate

00:09:19.125 --> 00:09:21.352
from the government and it's intended

00:09:21.352 --> 00:09:24.102
to be a kind of a non-political entity.

00:09:24.102 --> 00:09:25.949
But right now in Washington
you do have a debate

00:09:25.949 --> 00:09:28.052
where it looks like the
Biden administration wants

00:09:28.052 --> 00:09:30.102
to be more aggressive on the stimulus

00:09:30.102 --> 00:09:32.852
while the Republican
members of Congress wanna be

00:09:32.852 --> 00:09:34.052
a little bit more conservative.

00:09:34.052 --> 00:09:36.612
They also wanna do it
but we're talking about

00:09:36.612 --> 00:09:41.520
I think 600 billion versus
well, over a trillion or two

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:44.082
does the Federal Reserve
have a point of view on that?

00:09:44.082 --> 00:09:47.275
Or are you allowed to have a
public point of view on that?

00:09:47.275 --> 00:09:50.150
- Well, I get asked about that
from elected representatives

00:09:50.150 --> 00:09:51.276
from my district all the time

00:09:51.276 --> 00:09:54.012
and I always give them
the best advice I can.

00:09:54.012 --> 00:09:56.830
I think those kinds of
negotiations generally between

00:09:56.830 --> 00:10:00.262
where should be 1 million or
600 billion or 1.4 trillion?

00:10:00.262 --> 00:10:02.462
I think we try to stay out
of that because that really

00:10:02.462 --> 00:10:05.512
is about the political process
making those decisions.

00:10:05.512 --> 00:10:07.163
I think I've been very vocal

00:10:07.163 --> 00:10:09.192
and I'll continue to be vocal saying

00:10:10.132 --> 00:10:12.412
the job market today in America

00:10:12.412 --> 00:10:14.715
is as bad as the worst job market

00:10:14.715 --> 00:10:16.732
during the financial crisis.

00:10:16.732 --> 00:10:20.982
They have an unemployment
rate of 6.5, 6.7%.

00:10:20.982 --> 00:10:23.233
The real unemployment rate is around 10%,

00:10:23.233 --> 00:10:25.972
there's a lot of people have
given up looking for work.

00:10:25.972 --> 00:10:29.352
So my basic message is
we are nowhere close

00:10:29.352 --> 00:10:30.732
to having rebuilt our economy

00:10:30.732 --> 00:10:31.565
and putting people back to work,

00:10:31.565 --> 00:10:34.912
and so whatever Congress and
the administration choose to do

00:10:34.912 --> 00:10:37.249
I would encourage them to
focus on those millions

00:10:37.249 --> 00:10:39.912
of Americans that are
still suffering today,

00:10:39.912 --> 00:10:42.622
still out of work, they
need more assistance until

00:10:42.622 --> 00:10:44.372
we can get this pandemic behind us.

00:10:45.333 --> 00:10:46.447
- Yeah, and what you just highlighted

00:10:46.447 --> 00:10:47.434
I think is a really important point

00:10:47.434 --> 00:10:49.582
'cause a lot of folks don't realize that

00:10:49.582 --> 00:10:51.018
the details of how
things like unemployment

00:10:51.018 --> 00:10:54.071
how they're calculated really
tell you a lot about things,

00:10:54.071 --> 00:10:57.321
because there's a world where
people could get laid off

00:10:57.321 --> 00:10:59.962
and as long as they're
continuing to look for work

00:10:59.962 --> 00:11:02.292
they're included in the labor pool.

00:11:02.292 --> 00:11:04.822
And so they're counted
in the unemployment,

00:11:04.822 --> 00:11:06.592
but then if they just
give up at some point,

00:11:06.592 --> 00:11:08.163
say yeah, you know what? I'm
just not gonna find a job.

00:11:08.163 --> 00:11:11.158
I'm just gonna like live with
my parents or something else.

00:11:11.158 --> 00:11:13.947
Then they're not even
counted in the unemployment

00:11:13.947 --> 00:11:17.602
so actually you could have this dynamic

00:11:17.602 --> 00:11:19.652
but to your point, I guess,

00:11:19.652 --> 00:11:21.702
the more stimulus we can do the better.

00:11:21.702 --> 00:11:25.872
How concerned if we do
very aggressive stimulus

00:11:25.872 --> 00:11:27.652
'cause we talked about earlier

00:11:27.652 --> 00:11:29.402
and this is kind of your
classical point of view

00:11:29.402 --> 00:11:32.772
is if the federal government
runs huge deficits

00:11:32.772 --> 00:11:36.012
in order to provide stimulus all

00:11:36.012 --> 00:11:37.922
while interest rates are quite low

00:11:37.922 --> 00:11:40.182
that might create a lot of demand

00:11:40.182 --> 00:11:43.718
for a limited pool of resources
or capacity or productivity

00:11:43.718 --> 00:11:46.172
which could lead to a lot of inflation

00:11:46.172 --> 00:11:47.992
which is obviously really
to one of y'all's charter.

00:11:47.992 --> 00:11:51.062
How worried are you all
about that right now?

00:11:51.062 --> 00:11:52.062
- I'll speak for myself,

00:11:52.062 --> 00:11:52.992
I'm not worried about it.

00:11:52.992 --> 00:11:55.232
Inflation has been
running low for 10 years

00:11:55.232 --> 00:11:57.692
before the pandemic, and
now it's running even lower.

00:11:57.692 --> 00:11:58.753
If we had a little bit more inflation

00:11:58.753 --> 00:12:01.312
I think we would consider
that to be success

00:12:01.312 --> 00:12:04.582
in as achieving our goal of
2% inflation here in Europe.

00:12:04.582 --> 00:12:07.702
But something really curious
happened in this pandemic,

00:12:07.702 --> 00:12:09.771
those of us who kept our
jobs like you, like me

00:12:09.771 --> 00:12:12.702
like many of your viewers here today,

00:12:12.702 --> 00:12:14.221
generally speaking,
we're saving more money

00:12:14.221 --> 00:12:15.768
'cause we're not going out to restaurants

00:12:15.768 --> 00:12:16.999
except we're going to the grocery store.

00:12:16.999 --> 00:12:19.562
We're not taking vacations and trips,

00:12:19.562 --> 00:12:20.732
we're staying at home.

00:12:20.732 --> 00:12:23.422
So the savings rate has gone way up

00:12:23.422 --> 00:12:26.402
because people who kept
their jobs are doing fine,

00:12:26.402 --> 00:12:27.351
their incomes haven't been cut

00:12:27.351 --> 00:12:29.142
and they're saving more money.

00:12:29.142 --> 00:12:30.892
So the way the macro-economy works,

00:12:30.892 --> 00:12:32.922
if you keep that money in the bank

00:12:32.922 --> 00:12:35.562
it ends up flowing back
into the financial markets

00:12:35.562 --> 00:12:38.212
and that money is actually
available to financier,

00:12:38.212 --> 00:12:39.812
these increased government deficit.

00:12:39.812 --> 00:12:41.907
So I do think at some point

00:12:41.907 --> 00:12:43.621
we have to get our fiscal house in order,

00:12:43.621 --> 00:12:46.172
we have to make tough
choices as a country,

00:12:46.172 --> 00:12:48.432
but right now this is like we are at war

00:12:48.432 --> 00:12:51.042
and this is in my view war time spending

00:12:51.042 --> 00:12:53.132
to defeat the virus, that enemy

00:12:53.132 --> 00:12:54.772
and to support our fellow Americans

00:12:54.772 --> 00:12:55.732
until we can get through it.

00:12:55.732 --> 00:12:57.465
So we have the capacity to do it.

00:12:59.132 --> 00:13:02.502
- And I wanna encourage
everyone watching on YouTube

00:13:02.502 --> 00:13:03.335
or Facebook, please send your questions.

00:13:03.335 --> 00:13:04.602
We're already getting a
lot of great questions

00:13:04.602 --> 00:13:09.602
and I'll start with one from
SmartBear on YouTube asking

00:13:10.022 --> 00:13:12.246
how do you think the economy
will change in the near future

00:13:12.246 --> 00:13:14.121
due to the COVID-19 variants and more?

00:13:14.121 --> 00:13:17.832
and I'll extend that, do you
think there's gonna be...

00:13:17.832 --> 00:13:20.710
let's hope a year from now,
we're back to some normalcy.

00:13:20.710 --> 00:13:23.352
Do you think there's gonna
be structural changes

00:13:23.352 --> 00:13:24.382
to the economy because of what

00:13:24.382 --> 00:13:26.315
we're going through it with COVID?

00:13:27.522 --> 00:13:30.314
- I think that there are
hard to identify right now.

00:13:30.314 --> 00:13:32.547
I mean, one example is
we've all figured out,

00:13:32.547 --> 00:13:35.102
you figure this out Sal, a long time ago

00:13:35.102 --> 00:13:36.085
but we all the rest of us figured out

00:13:36.085 --> 00:13:38.972
that you can actually work
remotely very effectively

00:13:38.972 --> 00:13:42.282
in many industries and in many
cases, not all, but in many.

00:13:42.282 --> 00:13:45.532
And so I would expect
that the use of technology

00:13:45.532 --> 00:13:48.232
like this is going to
be permanently higher

00:13:48.232 --> 00:13:49.332
than it was before the pandemic.

00:13:49.332 --> 00:13:52.025
I don't know, we have 1100
employees in our bank,

00:13:52.972 --> 00:13:55.217
all the 50 or 70 of them
are working remotely,

00:13:55.217 --> 00:13:57.312
working from home.

00:13:57.312 --> 00:13:59.282
I expect the vast majority
to come into the bank

00:13:59.282 --> 00:14:01.762
but many structures look
a little bit different

00:14:01.762 --> 00:14:03.382
to take advantage of this technology

00:14:03.382 --> 00:14:04.932
and provide more flexibility.

00:14:04.932 --> 00:14:06.312
It's certainly possible.

00:14:06.312 --> 00:14:08.328
Might we travel a little
less for conferences

00:14:08.328 --> 00:14:10.722
and do more of them virtually

00:14:10.722 --> 00:14:12.972
or in a hybrid format certainly?

00:14:12.972 --> 00:14:14.964
So I think that likely
there'll be many changes

00:14:14.964 --> 00:14:17.642
that are permanent, that are positive

00:14:17.642 --> 00:14:20.069
that are hard to identify
exactly right now.

00:14:21.497 --> 00:14:23.808
- Yeah, I actually even heard,
I agree with a lot of that

00:14:23.808 --> 00:14:25.690
and I'm fascinated, even at Khan Academy

00:14:25.690 --> 00:14:28.709
we're thinking about what's
the right use of office space?

00:14:28.709 --> 00:14:31.362
How much travel do we need
to do? Et cetera, et cetera.

00:14:31.362 --> 00:14:33.252
But you know, I was entertained

00:14:33.252 --> 00:14:34.802
when I read, this was several months ago

00:14:34.802 --> 00:14:37.152
that retailers are seeing a lot of sales

00:14:37.152 --> 00:14:41.068
of tops, of formal tops
and casual bottoms.

00:14:41.068 --> 00:14:42.538
So finally the rest of the world

00:14:42.538 --> 00:14:43.568
is starting to dress like I am,

00:14:43.568 --> 00:14:44.880
I don't know if you're
wearing jogging pants

00:14:44.880 --> 00:14:46.762
below the waist, but I am right now.

00:14:46.762 --> 00:14:47.595
So (laughing).

00:14:47.595 --> 00:14:49.212
- I'm wearing (mumbles) you can't see

00:14:49.212 --> 00:14:51.397
if I'm wearing (mumbles).

00:14:51.397 --> 00:14:53.215
(laughing)

00:14:53.215 --> 00:14:55.022
- I won't make you validated

00:14:55.022 --> 00:14:56.958
but I said, oh, I'm going
to talk to the president

00:14:56.958 --> 00:14:58.328
of the Federal Reserve Bank of Minneapolis

00:14:58.328 --> 00:14:59.962
I have to put on a button shirt

00:14:59.962 --> 00:15:01.502
but I still have my jogging pants on,

00:15:01.502 --> 00:15:06.369
but yes there might be
another structural change.

00:15:06.369 --> 00:15:10.242
There's a question here
from YouTube on Chalk Day

00:15:10.242 --> 00:15:12.104
and I'll ask the question,
I'll extend it a little bit.

00:15:12.104 --> 00:15:14.812
Should the education system
teach banking at school?

00:15:14.812 --> 00:15:16.391
And I'll extent it should there be more

00:15:16.391 --> 00:15:21.282
of an emphasis on finance,
personal finance, accounting?

00:15:21.282 --> 00:15:24.392
A lot of people if you go
back to the financial crisis

00:15:24.392 --> 00:15:26.451
back in 2009, 2010 people described it

00:15:26.451 --> 00:15:28.292
to just lack of financial literacy,

00:15:28.292 --> 00:15:29.892
people getting over their heads.

00:15:32.403 --> 00:15:34.732
Obviously, at Khan Academy
will try to do what we can

00:15:34.732 --> 00:15:38.125
but I'm curious your point of
view on Chalk Day's question.

00:15:39.222 --> 00:15:41.852
- Absolutely, I think a bigger investment

00:15:41.852 --> 00:15:43.852
in financial literacy is just making,

00:15:43.852 --> 00:15:46.552
regardless of what your
profession is going to be

00:15:46.552 --> 00:15:49.092
you're gonna have to manage
your own personal finances

00:15:49.092 --> 00:15:50.952
and find a way to make ends meet

00:15:50.952 --> 00:15:53.012
and figure out can I afford this house

00:15:53.012 --> 00:15:55.902
or can I afford this car or
even this cell phone plan

00:15:55.902 --> 00:15:57.051
that I might be interested in?

00:15:57.051 --> 00:16:01.512
The basics of financial literacy
I think is really important

00:16:01.512 --> 00:16:02.345
and just leaving it to people,

00:16:02.345 --> 00:16:03.562
what do we do right now?

00:16:03.562 --> 00:16:04.993
For the vast majority
of people we just say,

00:16:04.993 --> 00:16:07.622
well you'll kind of
figure it out as you go,

00:16:07.622 --> 00:16:11.212
but then when you make mistakes
and you damage your credit

00:16:11.212 --> 00:16:13.042
it can be very hard to recover from that

00:16:13.042 --> 00:16:15.162
and then your interest rates are higher

00:16:15.162 --> 00:16:15.995
and then it makes it more likely

00:16:15.995 --> 00:16:18.462
that you're gonna run
into financial problems.

00:16:18.462 --> 00:16:19.762
So I think we'd all be better off

00:16:19.762 --> 00:16:22.962
if people had the basic skills
they needed at the front end

00:16:22.962 --> 00:16:25.712
so they don't have to kind
of learn by making mistakes.

00:16:26.932 --> 00:16:28.352
- Yeah, completely, completely agree

00:16:28.352 --> 00:16:29.803
and we're happy, we should figure out

00:16:29.803 --> 00:16:34.242
how we can work together
to make that a reality.

00:16:34.242 --> 00:16:36.202
Another great question from YouTube

00:16:36.202 --> 00:16:38.972
Christina Espinoza,
what advice do you have

00:16:38.972 --> 00:16:41.102
for teenagers who saw the GameStop event

00:16:41.102 --> 00:16:43.542
and wanna take advantage
of stock slash investing?

00:16:43.542 --> 00:16:44.742
What do children need to know?

00:16:44.742 --> 00:16:46.455
So I have an opinion there as well

00:16:46.455 --> 00:16:47.935
I know you are a former investment banker.

00:16:47.935 --> 00:16:50.252
I used to work at it as an
analyst at a hedge fund.

00:16:50.252 --> 00:16:51.085
So I definitely have a point of view,

00:16:51.085 --> 00:16:53.872
but yeah, what would you
tell to Christina about,

00:16:53.872 --> 00:16:54.705
well I'm just curious,

00:16:54.705 --> 00:16:56.009
what's your point of
view on the whole event?

00:16:56.009 --> 00:16:57.588
Is that, do you think this
is just gonna pass us by

00:16:57.588 --> 00:17:00.382
or do you think this
will lead to some form

00:17:00.382 --> 00:17:02.612
of structural change in some way?

00:17:02.612 --> 00:17:03.872
And then what advice would you have

00:17:03.872 --> 00:17:08.232
for teenagers who are looking
to get into finance or invest?

00:17:08.232 --> 00:17:09.264
- Well, I think what you're
seeing in the markets

00:17:09.264 --> 00:17:11.632
with these individual stocks like GameStop

00:17:11.632 --> 00:17:13.502
I would just call it a gamble.

00:17:13.502 --> 00:17:16.652
It's one group of speculators
that's battling another group

00:17:16.652 --> 00:17:19.142
of speculators in a test of wills,

00:17:19.142 --> 00:17:20.689
and my basic view on that is,

00:17:20.689 --> 00:17:21.850
that's their right, God bless them,

00:17:21.850 --> 00:17:24.377
let them do what they want and
if they make money, terrific,

00:17:24.377 --> 00:17:27.962
if they lose money, that's
their fault and that's on them

00:17:27.962 --> 00:17:29.226
and nobody needs to take
care of them for that.

00:17:29.226 --> 00:17:31.772
So they need to go into it eyes wide open,

00:17:31.772 --> 00:17:34.612
but to the teenager asking a question,

00:17:34.612 --> 00:17:36.546
that's gambling, it is rolling the dice

00:17:36.546 --> 00:17:38.525
and when, if you go to Vegas
and I used to go to Vegas

00:17:38.525 --> 00:17:41.986
when I was a lot younger, you
go to Vegas with some money

00:17:41.986 --> 00:17:44.522
in your pocket, you gotta
be prepared to walk away

00:17:44.522 --> 00:17:45.972
and lose it all 'cause that's

00:17:45.972 --> 00:17:48.281
what's possibly what could happen.

00:17:48.281 --> 00:17:49.786
If you're serious about
getting into investing

00:17:49.786 --> 00:17:50.978
you should study it.

00:17:50.978 --> 00:17:54.382
You should take some
classes, go to go to college

00:17:54.382 --> 00:17:56.812
or community college take
some finance classes,

00:17:56.812 --> 00:17:59.082
some banking classes
and you can learn about

00:17:59.082 --> 00:18:02.259
how financial markets work so
that you're not just blindly

00:18:02.259 --> 00:18:03.681
so that you're not just blindly gambling.

00:18:03.681 --> 00:18:05.272
What we're seeing right now,

00:18:05.272 --> 00:18:06.712
is just gambling in the markets

00:18:06.712 --> 00:18:08.492
and it might work out for some,

00:18:08.492 --> 00:18:10.515
it's not going to work
out for most people.

00:18:12.712 --> 00:18:15.797
- Yep, and I just wanna add,
I a hundred percent agree

00:18:15.797 --> 00:18:17.625
with everything you just said,

00:18:18.692 --> 00:18:21.142
as someone who used to work
in the investment world

00:18:21.142 --> 00:18:23.182
I can tell you that when it comes

00:18:23.182 --> 00:18:26.150
to individual stock picking
there's many very smart people

00:18:26.150 --> 00:18:28.352
who are doing a lot of research

00:18:28.352 --> 00:18:30.672
and still is a very hard job.

00:18:30.672 --> 00:18:32.712
And I remember my old
boss used to tell me,

00:18:32.712 --> 00:18:35.011
anytime you think you have
a good investment idea

00:18:35.011 --> 00:18:36.149
when you're buying a stock,

00:18:36.149 --> 00:18:38.722
someone else is selling that stock to you,

00:18:38.722 --> 00:18:40.512
so you've got to believe that

00:18:40.512 --> 00:18:43.212
you know something that they don't.

00:18:43.212 --> 00:18:46.652
And I do encourage anyone of any age

00:18:46.652 --> 00:18:49.292
but especially young people,
put some small amount

00:18:49.292 --> 00:18:51.319
of money into the stock market if you can

00:18:51.319 --> 00:18:53.387
and you will learn this lesson (laughs)

00:18:53.387 --> 00:18:56.732
that the market is a lot harder

00:18:56.732 --> 00:18:59.372
and the other people on
the side of the trade

00:18:59.372 --> 00:19:01.685
are probably more sophisticated
than you might guess,

00:19:01.685 --> 00:19:03.122
and it's so completely agree with you Neel

00:19:03.122 --> 00:19:05.249
that it is very speculative.

00:19:05.249 --> 00:19:07.090
Now there could be something
about accumulating capital

00:19:07.090 --> 00:19:11.352
have a ding of diversified
portfolio over time

00:19:11.352 --> 00:19:13.672
that could be something to look into

00:19:13.672 --> 00:19:15.322
or if you wanna become a professional

00:19:15.322 --> 00:19:16.332
as an investment manager.

00:19:16.332 --> 00:19:20.092
Yeah, do your research
and do your homework

00:19:20.092 --> 00:19:21.542
and learn to invest the right way

00:19:21.542 --> 00:19:23.822
not just speculation, but yeah--

00:19:23.822 --> 00:19:25.722
- It is [Indistinct].

00:19:25.722 --> 00:19:28.862
So for example, I don't
own any individual stocks,

00:19:28.862 --> 00:19:32.612
I own index funds so I do have
exposure to the stock market

00:19:32.612 --> 00:19:34.625
and the bond market, but
I'm not sitting here saying

00:19:34.625 --> 00:19:36.932
I'd have to spend my
whole day and night trying

00:19:36.932 --> 00:19:39.032
to do research to compete
with the professionals

00:19:39.032 --> 00:19:40.502
who are literally spending all day

00:19:40.502 --> 00:19:42.345
and all night doing that research.

00:19:43.442 --> 00:19:44.782
- Yeah, I'll just add to that

00:19:44.782 --> 00:19:47.661
I used to my day job for
seven years was an analyst

00:19:47.661 --> 00:19:51.442
at Hedge fund where all we
did is essentially pick stocks

00:19:51.442 --> 00:19:54.862
and like you, my 401k I have it

00:19:54.862 --> 00:19:57.282
in some diversified mutual funds

00:19:57.282 --> 00:19:59.588
but I own no individual
stocks right now (laughs)

00:19:59.588 --> 00:20:02.641
all of our savings I bought
some rental properties

00:20:02.641 --> 00:20:05.342
(laughs) that my say.

00:20:05.342 --> 00:20:06.475
So I completely agree with you,

00:20:06.475 --> 00:20:10.862
it's a very hard game to
pick individual, individual,

00:20:10.862 --> 00:20:13.592
individual stocks for things like that.

00:20:13.592 --> 00:20:16.682
One question that I like to ask guests,

00:20:16.682 --> 00:20:18.562
because we could talk
about the economy all day

00:20:18.562 --> 00:20:20.082
and I have a ton of questions there

00:20:20.082 --> 00:20:22.552
but also just wanna understand
your personal journey

00:20:22.552 --> 00:20:24.652
to how you got there, cause I don't think,

00:20:25.772 --> 00:20:27.732
I'm guessing when you were 15 years old,

00:20:27.732 --> 00:20:30.012
you didn't say oh my
career is going to be,

00:20:30.012 --> 00:20:30.845
I'm going to be president of

00:20:30.845 --> 00:20:32.772
the Federal Reserve Bank of Minneapolis,

00:20:32.772 --> 00:20:34.513
and I know there's a lot of young people

00:20:34.513 --> 00:20:36.722
who are constantly like what
should I do with my life?

00:20:36.722 --> 00:20:38.602
How do I have an impact?

00:20:38.602 --> 00:20:39.498
So what was your journey like?

00:20:39.498 --> 00:20:40.771
What did you think you were going to be

00:20:40.771 --> 00:20:42.302
when you were a teenager?

00:20:42.302 --> 00:20:43.135
And then how did that evolve?

00:20:43.135 --> 00:20:45.432
How did you end up where you are?

00:20:45.432 --> 00:20:48.142
- Well, I went to college and I studied

00:20:48.142 --> 00:20:49.751
mechanical engineering at
the university of Illinois.

00:20:49.751 --> 00:20:51.802
And the reason I went
to engineering school

00:20:51.802 --> 00:20:53.814
is 'cause as a kid I always loved math

00:20:53.814 --> 00:20:55.730
and science and physics
and building things

00:20:55.730 --> 00:20:59.072
so engineering seemed like an
obvious place for me to go.

00:20:59.072 --> 00:21:00.414
I did my bachelor's and
master's in engineering,

00:21:00.414 --> 00:21:01.790
and then I went into aerospace

00:21:01.790 --> 00:21:04.682
and started developing
technology for NASA.

00:21:04.682 --> 00:21:08.212
I went there because to me
working on NASA space center

00:21:08.212 --> 00:21:11.562
was as exciting as
challenging as it could be,

00:21:11.562 --> 00:21:14.168
and so I learned a ton and I
loved it, but I also realized

00:21:14.168 --> 00:21:16.982
that my managers wanted me to get my PhD.

00:21:16.982 --> 00:21:18.382
In which case I'd really be committing

00:21:18.382 --> 00:21:19.712
to a career in research.

00:21:19.712 --> 00:21:22.032
I wasn't ready to do that
so I decided to go back

00:21:22.032 --> 00:21:24.632
to business school to learn
about business and finance,

00:21:24.632 --> 00:21:25.671
that led me to banking.

00:21:25.671 --> 00:21:27.028
Then I went to the US treasury department

00:21:27.028 --> 00:21:29.322
and I've had a lot of twists and turns.

00:21:29.322 --> 00:21:31.437
The one common thread for
me is I always pursue things

00:21:31.437 --> 00:21:34.072
I'm really interested in
that I think are important

00:21:34.072 --> 00:21:35.892
that I think are challenging.

00:21:35.892 --> 00:21:36.725
And if I'm really challenged

00:21:36.725 --> 00:21:39.372
and I'm interested I'll
end up doing my best

00:21:39.372 --> 00:21:41.521
and that'll lead to hopefully
lead me to be successful

00:21:41.521 --> 00:21:43.052
and that's been true so far.

00:21:43.052 --> 00:21:44.982
So there's been no plan,

00:21:44.982 --> 00:21:46.672
I've done a variety of different things.

00:21:46.672 --> 00:21:48.270
The longest job I've
ever had is now here at

00:21:48.270 --> 00:21:50.482
the Minneapolis Fed for five years

00:21:50.482 --> 00:21:51.796
and I'm excited I just got reappointed,

00:21:51.796 --> 00:21:55.452
so I'll be staying around,
which I'm excited about.

00:21:55.452 --> 00:21:57.131
But I guess my message is pursue things

00:21:57.131 --> 00:22:00.342
you're genuinely interested
in and life is long

00:22:00.342 --> 00:22:02.722
your first job does not
have to be your last job.

00:22:02.722 --> 00:22:04.018
Sal as you said you were an analyst

00:22:04.018 --> 00:22:06.082
at a Hedge fund for seven years.

00:22:06.082 --> 00:22:07.134
I bet along the way you never dreamt

00:22:07.134 --> 00:22:10.306
that you'd be running
this a not-for-profit

00:22:10.306 --> 00:22:12.606
educating millions of
people around the world.

00:22:13.744 --> 00:22:17.535
- I had a lot of daydreams,
some of which came true.

00:22:17.535 --> 00:22:18.802
(laughing)

00:22:18.802 --> 00:22:20.345
Some of my--

00:22:20.345 --> 00:22:23.072
(Neel Kashkari mumbling)

00:22:23.072 --> 00:22:25.602
- Yeah, I still have my
whole singer songwriter

00:22:25.602 --> 00:22:28.492
folk music career has still
not gotten off the ground,

00:22:28.492 --> 00:22:33.492
so it's all one platform onto the next

00:22:33.622 --> 00:22:36.252
but yeah, I couldn't agree
more with that narrative.

00:22:36.252 --> 00:22:38.537
I am curious and this might be part

00:22:38.537 --> 00:22:39.632
of your distant memory now

00:22:39.632 --> 00:22:43.482
but you definitely came first
on my radar back in 2010

00:22:43.482 --> 00:22:44.889
when it looked like the financial system

00:22:44.889 --> 00:22:47.622
was kind of seizing in some way,

00:22:47.622 --> 00:22:51.522
you were working for secretary
of treasury, Hank Paulson

00:22:51.522 --> 00:22:53.522
at the time to really think about how

00:22:53.522 --> 00:22:58.152
to unstick the economy is the
best way I can interpret it.

00:22:58.152 --> 00:23:01.652
But I do have to say when it was happening

00:23:01.652 --> 00:23:04.622
as much as I wanted the
economy to get unstuck

00:23:04.622 --> 00:23:06.206
I was also like, wow,
there's a lot of actors

00:23:06.206 --> 00:23:09.292
who did a lot of things that were shady.

00:23:09.292 --> 00:23:11.992
You had the ratings
agencies that were approving

00:23:11.992 --> 00:23:15.772
putting a high rating on
sliced and diced securities

00:23:15.772 --> 00:23:17.785
that were questionable in value

00:23:17.785 --> 00:23:19.572
and you had all these incentives.

00:23:19.572 --> 00:23:22.949
I think most of us even though
I now run a not-for-profit

00:23:22.949 --> 00:23:27.949
and you run a semi-quasi
independent government institution

00:23:28.932 --> 00:23:31.932
I would say we both arguably
believe in free markets

00:23:31.932 --> 00:23:34.012
and part of the best corrective force

00:23:34.012 --> 00:23:36.904
on free markets is kind of you deal

00:23:36.904 --> 00:23:39.272
with the consequences of your actions.

00:23:39.272 --> 00:23:42.562
And so back in 2010 when a lot
of these banks were kind of

00:23:42.562 --> 00:23:45.742
at the cusp of failing
because they'd argue

00:23:45.742 --> 00:23:49.362
they had taken on more
risk than they shouldn't.

00:23:49.362 --> 00:23:50.972
How did you all think about that?

00:23:50.972 --> 00:23:53.092
I used to say, yeah, I get you
need to keep the banks kind

00:23:53.092 --> 00:23:55.142
of going so the economy doesn't cease

00:23:55.142 --> 00:23:58.002
but what if you were to take the shares

00:23:58.002 --> 00:24:00.732
and kind of go through a
controlled bankruptcy process

00:24:00.732 --> 00:24:02.592
and distribute them to
the people of America

00:24:02.592 --> 00:24:04.492
so it stays private.

00:24:04.492 --> 00:24:06.362
What were the conversations back then?

00:24:06.362 --> 00:24:08.102
Around stuff like that?

00:24:08.102 --> 00:24:10.622
- Yeah, we had those conversations
and it was terrifying,

00:24:10.622 --> 00:24:12.202
and the fear that we had was that

00:24:12.202 --> 00:24:14.362
if the banking system collapsed

00:24:14.362 --> 00:24:17.162
and we'd have that justice moment,

00:24:17.162 --> 00:24:19.542
shareholder would get wiped
out, the banks would collapse.

00:24:19.542 --> 00:24:22.127
People said it was like a
forest fire, let the fire rage

00:24:22.127 --> 00:24:25.410
and then the next season,
new saplings emerge.

00:24:25.410 --> 00:24:28.218
And what I always point
out is that may be true

00:24:28.218 --> 00:24:30.412
but then all the animals will die

00:24:30.412 --> 00:24:31.695
and so what are the consequences

00:24:31.695 --> 00:24:35.402
to the American people who did
not cause the banking crisis

00:24:35.402 --> 00:24:38.514
if the US banking system
were allowed to collapse.

00:24:38.514 --> 00:24:41.982
The great depression
we had 25% unemployment

00:24:41.982 --> 00:24:44.792
in the great recession,
even though we did all

00:24:44.792 --> 00:24:48.532
of this massive intervention,
treasury Congress, the Fed,

00:24:48.532 --> 00:24:49.824
we still had 10% unemployment.

00:24:49.824 --> 00:24:51.917
So it was still a devastating crisis,

00:24:51.917 --> 00:24:55.422
and so I don't think letting it all fail

00:24:55.422 --> 00:24:57.533
was a reasonable thing
to do because the cost

00:24:57.533 --> 00:25:00.782
would have been born
by the American people

00:25:00.782 --> 00:25:02.702
not just those on wall street.

00:25:02.702 --> 00:25:05.752
Having said that, I've
been a strong advocate

00:25:05.752 --> 00:25:07.652
since I joined the Minneapolis
Fed that we need to do

00:25:07.652 --> 00:25:10.442
a lot more to make sure
that can never happen again.

00:25:10.442 --> 00:25:14.902
And we need much stronger
regulations on the biggest banks

00:25:14.902 --> 00:25:16.662
so that they don't have risk

00:25:16.662 --> 00:25:18.282
of bringing down the US economy again,

00:25:18.282 --> 00:25:19.882
and we have not done enough yet.

00:25:21.662 --> 00:25:24.744
- Neel, I've actually come
around to that back in 2009, 2010

00:25:24.744 --> 00:25:26.582
I had a much more principled view

00:25:26.582 --> 00:25:29.332
that like, deal with the consequences

00:25:29.332 --> 00:25:32.182
or control the consequences
and figure out something

00:25:32.182 --> 00:25:33.208
that on a principle level.

00:25:33.208 --> 00:25:35.962
But I think you're
right, history has shown

00:25:35.962 --> 00:25:39.452
that what could have led us
into the next great depression

00:25:39.452 --> 00:25:42.642
those 10 years were actually a period

00:25:42.642 --> 00:25:45.832
of actually quite good economic growth

00:25:45.832 --> 00:25:47.619
that frankly if COVID didn't happen,

00:25:47.619 --> 00:25:49.762
we would have still been on that curve

00:25:49.762 --> 00:25:51.412
that started back in 2010.

00:25:51.412 --> 00:25:55.725
So, I've come around to the fact that

00:25:55.725 --> 00:25:57.652
I think y'all probably did save

00:25:57.652 --> 00:26:00.642
the economy (laughs) in that timeframe,

00:26:00.642 --> 00:26:03.976
and honestly the country
owes y'all for that

00:26:03.976 --> 00:26:06.618
'cause I can imagine
what has been through.

00:26:06.618 --> 00:26:08.832
Just in the remaining time that we have,

00:26:08.832 --> 00:26:10.462
I just kinda open it up to you,

00:26:10.462 --> 00:26:13.109
any advice you have for folks on,

00:26:13.109 --> 00:26:17.072
they're trying to form a view
on the economy, on the world

00:26:17.072 --> 00:26:18.562
or just on what to do with their life,

00:26:18.562 --> 00:26:21.692
where we have people of all
ages watching us right now.

00:26:21.692 --> 00:26:24.152
What advice would you have?

00:26:24.152 --> 00:26:26.439
- Well, first thing, get vaccinated

00:26:26.439 --> 00:26:28.242
as soon as you're eligible.

00:26:28.242 --> 00:26:31.172
I know as soon as my turn becomes,

00:26:31.172 --> 00:26:34.336
I'm 47 years old, I'm not
planning not to get a vaccine

00:26:34.336 --> 00:26:36.075
for a couple more months but
as soon as it's available,

00:26:36.075 --> 00:26:37.782
I'm gonna get vaccinated.

00:26:37.782 --> 00:26:40.282
The best thing we can do
to get our economy reopened

00:26:40.282 --> 00:26:42.912
is to vaccinate the vast
majority of Americans,

00:26:42.912 --> 00:26:44.982
starting with those who are highest risk

00:26:44.982 --> 00:26:46.322
but everybody else matters too

00:26:46.322 --> 00:26:47.662
because we could still get sick,

00:26:47.662 --> 00:26:48.822
we could still spread the virus

00:26:48.822 --> 00:26:51.352
and so that's so important
for the economic recovery.

00:26:51.352 --> 00:26:53.502
My mom has been vaccinated, my sister,

00:26:53.502 --> 00:26:56.453
my cousins they're doctors,
they've been vaccinated.

00:26:56.453 --> 00:26:58.508
I'm waiting for my turn,
so that's number one.

00:26:58.508 --> 00:27:01.639
Number two, and I just
applaud you for tuning in

00:27:01.639 --> 00:27:05.872
into this video call, or this podcast.

00:27:05.872 --> 00:27:06.843
Education is so important,

00:27:06.843 --> 00:27:09.255
it's been so important
to my life and my success

00:27:09.255 --> 00:27:11.702
and in our economy education

00:27:11.702 --> 00:27:13.482
is only becoming more important.

00:27:13.482 --> 00:27:16.037
It's important to continue
to improve your skills,

00:27:16.037 --> 00:27:17.292
continue to get new skills,

00:27:17.292 --> 00:27:20.078
and so keep investing in
yourself to get more skills

00:27:20.078 --> 00:27:21.892
and more education.

00:27:21.892 --> 00:27:22.972
Education is the key

00:27:22.972 --> 00:27:25.522
to unlocking opportunity in our country.

00:27:25.522 --> 00:27:28.922
It is the best tool we have
to equalize our society

00:27:28.922 --> 00:27:30.092
and we know it, and the reason

00:27:30.092 --> 00:27:31.962
I'm so passionate about education,

00:27:31.962 --> 00:27:34.472
if people get a good education
that's the most powerful tool

00:27:34.472 --> 00:27:36.002
we have to break the cycle of poverty.

00:27:36.002 --> 00:27:37.490
And so Sal, I really applaud what you

00:27:37.490 --> 00:27:39.922
and your colleagues at the
Khan Academy are doing.

00:27:39.922 --> 00:27:43.553
And I would just encourage all
of your viewers to be part of

00:27:43.553 --> 00:27:47.762
that process, invest in themselves,
invest in their families

00:27:47.762 --> 00:27:50.179
and the whole country
will be better off for it.

00:27:52.082 --> 00:27:54.132
- Well, I could agree
more about the vaccination

00:27:54.132 --> 00:27:56.362
and the education side of things,

00:27:56.362 --> 00:27:58.842
and in my household my
wife is a physician,

00:27:58.842 --> 00:27:59.742
so she got vaccinated.

00:27:59.742 --> 00:28:03.022
My mother-in-law who's at
risk needs a second dose

00:28:03.022 --> 00:28:06.155
but I'm now the missing link

00:28:06.155 --> 00:28:07.612
but I completely agree with that,

00:28:07.612 --> 00:28:10.742
and Neel thank you so much for joining us,

00:28:10.742 --> 00:28:12.952
so many questions have been coming in

00:28:12.952 --> 00:28:14.662
I apologize for not being able to get

00:28:14.662 --> 00:28:16.762
to a lot of them for everyone watching.

00:28:16.762 --> 00:28:18.042
But this was a real treat not only

00:28:18.042 --> 00:28:20.312
to understand the economy
a little bit better

00:28:20.312 --> 00:28:21.680
but also to understand your life journey

00:28:21.680 --> 00:28:24.212
and I think it gives everyone confidence

00:28:24.212 --> 00:28:27.372
that there's some really
good people trying to do

00:28:27.372 --> 00:28:29.634
a good for the world in our government

00:28:29.634 --> 00:28:31.122
and our financial system.

00:28:31.122 --> 00:28:33.114
So, thank you so much.

00:28:33.114 --> 00:28:35.764
- Thank you Sal, it is a
lot of fun, I appreciate it.

00:28:37.302 --> 00:28:38.135
- Thanks.

00:28:38.135 --> 00:28:39.532
So thank you everyone for joining,

00:28:39.532 --> 00:28:41.352
hopefully you enjoyed that conversation

00:28:41.352 --> 00:28:42.992
and I do encourage you there's a lot

00:28:42.992 --> 00:28:43.825
of questions that came in,

00:28:43.825 --> 00:28:46.700
there is one from Adrian Hemmer CEO,

00:28:46.700 --> 00:28:50.718
talking about where to invest
money and things like that.

00:28:50.718 --> 00:28:53.342
We actually do have a lot
of content on Khan Academy

00:28:53.342 --> 00:28:56.152
on both personal finance and on finance

00:28:56.152 --> 00:28:57.472
and capital markets generally.

00:28:57.472 --> 00:28:58.813
So if you're a young person,

00:28:58.813 --> 00:29:01.082
or if you're the parent of a young person,

00:29:01.082 --> 00:29:02.805
and watch that stuff
together because frankly,

00:29:02.805 --> 00:29:05.752
a lot of adults, most adults
don't understand exactly

00:29:05.752 --> 00:29:06.692
how all of that works.

00:29:06.692 --> 00:29:08.122
And so once you understand that,

00:29:08.122 --> 00:29:11.226
I'd also recommend you can
get a stock simulators,

00:29:11.226 --> 00:29:12.376
a lot of people are asking how

00:29:12.376 --> 00:29:15.102
to learn about stock investing
and things like that.

00:29:15.102 --> 00:29:17.152
You can play with fake money initially

00:29:17.152 --> 00:29:19.632
and see how you can perform,

00:29:19.632 --> 00:29:22.242
but if you have some money saved up $500

00:29:22.242 --> 00:29:25.732
a thousand dollars that could
be a really good education

00:29:25.732 --> 00:29:28.382
to kind of see, to start
learning about investing

00:29:28.382 --> 00:29:29.690
and putting it to work
and seeing what works

00:29:29.690 --> 00:29:32.542
and what doesn't work
I think is a good way

00:29:32.542 --> 00:29:34.467
to start learning about money.

